  "query_preview": "..A trough moving eastward across the state early this\nmorning is producing scattered rain and snow showers over eastern\nArizona. Most of the activity is in Navajo and Apache counties.\nThis is forecast to persist through mid morning before shifting\ninto New Mexico. Snow levels are dropping to 5500-6500 feet, with\nsome light accumulations (<1\") in a few areas and up to 1-2\" for \nthe higher elevations of the White Mountains. Some localized snow\nbands could produce 1-2\" of snow even at lower elevations from\nShow Low to St Johns southward, but it is uncertain exactly where\nthese will set up. Some slick roads may develop in these areas\nthis morning, use caution. Expect clearing skies and cool\ntemperatures this afternoon, with light northerly winds. Low\ntemperatures Sunday morning are likely to be the coldest so far\nthis season for most areas, teens in the usual high country cold\nspots, and 20s down to 4500 feet elevation.\n\nSunday - brief ridging will bring a slightly warmer afternoon with\nsome high clouds and light southerly winds. For the remainder of\nthe week - a couple of troughs are forecast to move across the\nregion, one on Monday and another on Wednesday. The Monday system\nis trending farther north, with just a slight chance for showers\nin far northern Arizona. There is more uncertainty in the track of\nthe Wednesday system, but very few ensemble members show any\nprecipitation in our area. Thanksgiving and Friday are looking dry\nat this time, with high temperatures slightly above normal.",
